\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb6 \strokec7 1:15\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 \strokec2 Traditional OCR systems worked by segmenting documents into individual characters\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb6 \strokec7 1:19\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 \strokec2 and then classifying each character using supervised learning.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb6 \strokec7 1:23\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 \strokec2 These systems could extract characters accurately,\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb6 \strokec7 1:25\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 \strokec2 but they lacked understanding of how\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf7 \cb6 \strokec7 1:27\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb6 \strokec2 different parts of a document hang together,\cb1 \
